    "text": "To determine how much money John spent on his computer, we need to account for the initial cost of the gaming PC, the amount he received from selling the old video card, and the cost of the new video card. Let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Initial cost of the gaming PC**: $1200\n2. **Amount received from selling the old video card**: $300\n3. **Cost of the new video card**: $500\n\nFirst, we calculate the net cost of the new video card after selling the old one:\n\\[ \\text{Net cost of the new video card} = \\text{Cost of the new video card} - \\text{Amount received from selling the old video card} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Net cost of the new video card} = 500 - 300 = 200 \\]\n\nNext, we add the net cost of the new video card to the initial cost of the gaming PC to find the total amount spent:\n\\[ \\text{Total amount spent} = \\text{Initial cost of the gaming PC} + \\text{Net cost of the new video card} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Total amount spent} = 1200 + 200 = 1400 \\]\n\nTherefore, the total amount of money John spent on his computer is \\(\\boxed{1400}\\).",
